{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's mechanism claims heterogeneous incentives and stacks 'naturally diversify the attack surface' and that 'decentralized services have different bugs, so a single exploit does not cascade.' But this merely assumes the independence whose distinguishing evidence the problem explicitly asks for. It provides no test, observation, or evidentiary criterion to tell truly independent failures from merely-appearing-uncorrelated ones. Its counterconditions (cost-herding, natural monopoly) are operational failure modes, not evidentiary distinctions. It thus answers the resilience argument but ignores the second, evidentiary clause of the problem.","target":"66da801c2ed8e97fcb0ad37f73f523fcc402677fa1b2c2e001c259d6e1c589c4"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"820c19a76dbb360a4dd5a1dfa008f64154e3e903813aab8c03883b6a38f1eb47"},{"attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts the large service is 'strictly more resilient' because internal redundancy 'eliminates single points of failure,' but its own countercondition admits the monolith's unified control plane can be a single point of failure. A system that can exhibit a single point of failure cannot have 'eliminated' single points of failure. The unconditional 'strictly more resilient' claim is thus directly contradicted by a conceded failure mode, and no condition is placed on the claim to restrict it to architectures lacking a unified control plane.","target":"901f19bce60a9e7af3cd077ebcfdc89b28ced362deb5f784e39d4693b88138d9"},{"attack":true,"case":"The analogy's overturn conditions include proving the decentralized network's shared-cause failure rate is lower than the monolith's internal coordination failure rate. But a lower failure rate is not the same as lower resilience to correlated failure: the problem asks which architecture is more resilient to correlated failure and how to distinguish truly independent from merely-appearing-uncorrelated failures. The target offers rate-comparison thresholds without identifying what evidence distinguishes genuine independence from hidden shared causes, so the evidentiary clause is unmet even when the analogy stands.","target":"abbfdcc32cecf4ebae9aea9bb636c60f03fcf04d44370a80b644633821ea94a5"}]}